[[Lennon–McCartney|Lennon and McCartney]] started composing "She Loves You" on 26 June 1963 after a concert at the [[NX Newcastle#History|Majestic Ballroom]] in [[Newcastle upon Tyne]] during their tour with [[Roy Orbison]] and [[Gerry and the Pacemakers]]. They began writing the song on the tour bus, and continued later that night at their hotel in Newcastle,{{sfn|Harry|2000|pp=990–992}}{{refn|group=nb|In 2003, plans to install a [[Commemorative plaque|plaque]] at the hotel were stalled, when it transpired that surviving Beatles [[Paul McCartney]] and [[Ringo Starr]] were unable to recall whether the group had stayed at the Imperial Hotel or Royal Turk's Head (although it was reported that a taxi driver remembered dropping them off at the latter in 1963).<ref>{{cite news |last=Davis |first=Laura |date=26 February 2003 |title=She Loves You (Where? Where? Where?) |url=http://www.liverpooldailypost.co.uk/liverpool-news/regional-news/2003/02/26/she-loves-you-where-where-where-92534-12677905/ |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130708163122/http://www.liverpooldailypost.co.uk/liverpool-news/regional-news/2003/02/26/she-loves-you-where-where-where-92534-12677905/ |archive-date=8 July 2013 |newspaper=[[Liverpool Daily Post]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last=Morton |first=David |date=20 September 2022 |title=Dinner time at one of Newcastle's most famous former hotels 45 years ago |url=https://www.chroniclelive.co.uk/news/history/dinner-time-one-newcastles-most-25061315 |website=ChronicleLive |location=Newcastle upon Tyne |publisher=Trinity Mirror North East |access-date=22 September 2022}}</ref>}} eventually completing it the following day at McCartney's family home in [[20 Forthlin Road|Forthlin Road]], [[Liverpool]].{{sfn|<!--title=-->The Beatles|2000|p=96}}

In 2000, McCartney said the initial idea for the song began with [[Bobby Rydell]]'s hit [[Forget Him (Bobby Rydell song)|"Forget Him"]] with its [[Call and response (music)|call and response pattern]], and that "as often happens, you think of one song when you write another{{nbsp}}[...] I'd planned an 'answering song' where a couple of us would sing 'she loves you' and the other ones would answer 'yeah yeah'. We decided that was a crummy idea but at least we then had the idea of a song called 'She Loves You'. So we sat in the hotel bedroom for a few hours and wrote it – John and I, sitting on twin beds with guitars."

Like many early Beatles songs, the title of "She Loves You" was framed around the use of [[personal pronoun]]s.{{sfn|Lewisohn|1988|p=9}} However, unusually for a love song, the lyrics are not about the narrator's love for someone else; instead the narrator functions as a helpful go-between for estranged lovers:

{{poemquote|
You think you lost your love,
Well, I saw her yesterday.
It's you she's thinking of –
And she told me what to say.
She says she loves you ...}}

This idea was attributed by Lennon to McCartney in 1980: "It was Paul's idea: instead of singing 'I love you' again, we'd have a third party. That kind of little detail is still in his work. He will write a story about someone. I'm more inclined to write about myself."{{sfn|The Beatles|2000|p=96}}

