| type = music
| filename = Shelovesyousample.ogg
| title = "She Loves You" sample
}}
Lennon, being mindful of [[Elvis Presley]]'s "[[All Shook Up]]", wanted something equally stirring: "I don't know where the 'yeah yeah yeah' came from [but] I remember when Elvis did 'All Shook Up' it was the first time in my life that I had heard 'uh huh', 'oh yeah', and 'yeah yeah' all sung in the same song".{{sfn|MacDonald|1997|p=74}} The song also included a number of [[falsetto]] "wooooo"s, which Lennon acknowledged as being inspired by [[the Isley Brothers]]' recording of "[[Twist and Shout]]",{{sfn|Badman|2000|p=65}} which the Beatles had earlier recorded, and which had also been inserted into the group's previous single, "[[From Me to You]]".{{sfn|Schaffner|1977|pp=21–22}} As Lennon later said: "We stuck it in everything".{{sfn|Badman|2000|p=65}} McCartney recalls them playing the finished song on acoustic guitars to his father Jim at home immediately after the song was completed: "We went into the living room and said 'Dad, listen to this. What do you think? And he said 'That's very nice son, but there's enough of these Americanisms around. Couldn't you sing 'She loves you, yes, yes, yes!' At which point we collapsed in a heap and said 'No, Dad, you don't quite get it!'"{{sfn|Miles|1997|p=150}} EMI recording engineer [[Norman Smith (record producer)|Norman Smith]] had a somewhat similar reaction, later recounting, "I was setting up the microphone when I first saw the lyrics on the music stand, 'She loves you, yeah, yeah, yeah, She loves you, yeah, yeah, yeah, She loves you, yeah, yeah, yeah, Yeah!' I thought, Oh my God, what a lyric! This is going to be one that I ''do not'' like. But when they started to sing it – bang, wow, terrific, I was up at the mixer jogging around."{{sfn|Lewisohn|1988|p=32}}

The "yeah, yeah, yeah" refrain proved an immediate, infectious [[Hook (music)|musical hook]].{{sfn|Carr|Tyler|1975|p=20}}{{sfn|Starr|2007|p=181}} Unusually, the song starts with the hook right away, instead of introducing it after a verse or two.{{sfn|Starr|2007|p=181}} "She Loves You" does not include a bridge, instead using the refrain to join the various verses. The chords tend to change every two measures, and the harmonic scheme is mostly static.

The arrangement starts with a two-count from Starr on the drums,{{sfn|Carr|Tyler|1975|p=20}} and his fills are an important part of the record throughout.{{sfn|Schaffner|1977|p=22}} The electric instruments are mixed higher than before, especially McCartney's bass, adding to the sense of musical power that the record provides.{{sfn|Schaffner|1977|p=22}}{{sfn|Carr|Tyler|1975|p=20}} The lead vocal is sung by Lennon and McCartney, switching between unison and harmony.{{sfn|Carr|Tyler|1975|p=20}}{{sfn|Castleman|Podrazik|1975|p=164}}

[[George Martin]], the Beatles' producer, questioned the validity of the major [[sixth chord]] that ends the song, an idea suggested by [[George Harrison]].{{sfn|Harry|2000|p=601}} "They sort of finished on this curious singing chord which was a major sixth, with George [Harrison] doing the sixth and the others doing the third and fifth in the chord. It was just like a [[Glenn Miller]] arrangement."{{sfn|Badman|2000|p=65}} The device had also been used by country music-influenced artists in the 1950s.{{sfn|Starr|2007|p=181}} McCartney later reflected: "We took it to George Martin and sang 'She loves you, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeeeeeaah ...' and that tight little sixth cluster we had at the end. George [Martin] said: 'It's very corny, I would never end on a sixth'. But we said 'It's such a great sound, it doesn't matter'."{{sfn|The Beatles|2000|p=96}} ''The Beatles: Complete Scores'' shows only the notes D (the fifth) and E (the sixth) sung for the final chord,{{sfn|The Beatles: Complete Scores|1993|p=872}} while on the recording McCartney sang G (the root) as Harrison sang E and Lennon sang D.{{sfn|Everett|2001|p=175}}

In the opinion of [[Roger McGuinn]] of [[the Byrds]], writers who attempt to define the origins of [[folk rock]] "don't realise that the Beatles were responsible as far back as 1963". He cites "She Loves You" as one of the first examples where the Beatles introduced folk chord changes into rock music and so initiated the new genre.<ref>Alexander, Phil; et al. (July 2006). "The 101 Greatest Beatles Songs". [[Mojo (magazine)|Mojo]]. pp. 92–93.</ref> These songs were all influential in providing a template for successfully assimilating folk-based [[chord progressions]] and melodies into pop music.

